<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brzeg</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Brieg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Brzeg is a town on the Oder in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opole">Opole</a> Voivodeship of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. From 1311 it was the capital of the Duchy of Brzeg, ruled by Silesian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piast-dynasty">Piasts</a> who rebuilt its castle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> style, earning it the name of the Silesian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wawel-hill">Wawel</a>. After the last Piast died in 1675 it passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and in 1742 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, remaining German as Brieg until 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/brzeg.jpg" alt="Brzeg" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stary1995 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brzeg,_Poland_-_panoramio_(45).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
